"event_title"} "Javelin Throw, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "26 – 28 August 2004"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/779"} "Olympiako Stadio, Olympiako Athletiko Kentro Athinon Spiros Loues, Maroussi"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/66273"} "33 from 23 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "American " [:a {:href "/athletes/89130"} "Breaux Greer"] " led the qualifying with 87.25 (286-3) but injured his always sensitive knee in the process and would finish last in the final. The favorites were " [:a {:href "/athletes/66875"} "Ján Železný"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/68853"} "Steve Backley"] " again, along with Russian " [:a {:href "/athletes/75422"} "Sergey Makarov"] ", the 2003 World Champion. Železný and Backley had had great careers but were now past their prime, Železný finishing ninth and Backley fourth, never in the medals. Makarov also did not have his best day, only able to get the bronze medal. In round two, Norway’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/107086"} "Andreas Thorkildsen"] " took the lead with 86.50 (283-9½) and that would be good enough for the gold medal. Nobody else could get over 85 metres. He had no major international credentials, although in 2001 he had set the world junior record with 83.87 (275-2). " [:em {} "Track &amp; Field News"] " quoted Thorkildsen as saying, “If you had said the words ‘Olympic champion’ to me yesterday, I wouldn’t have believed you. But now it sounds great.”"] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Qualifying"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "2626"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/107086"} "Andreas Thorkildsen"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/NOR"} [:img {:style "paddi